  <h1 class="hl">The Farms of<br>the Future</h1>
  <div class="deck">How much space do you need to grow 10,000 heads of lettuce a day? Not that much, it turns out.</div>

  <div class="strip">
    <div class="cell"><div class="ph" style="height:26mm" data-label="08-shelves"><img src="images/08-shelves.jpg" alt=""></div></div>
    <div class="cell"><div class="ph" style="height:26mm" data-label="08-ledlights"><img src="images/08-ledlights.jpg" alt=""></div></div>
    <div class="cell"><div class="ph" style="height:26mm" data-label="08-lettuce"><img src="images/08-lettuce.jpg" alt=""></div></div>
  </div>

  <div class="body-txt" style="column-count:2;column-gap:9mm;margin-bottom:11px">
    <p>On an indoor farm in Miyagi, Japan, farmers grow that much lettuce every day — in a building smaller than half a football pitch. They grow it on <strong>shelves</strong>, up to fifteen levels high. Special LED lights help it grow faster.</p>
    <p>Indoor farms use <strong>less</strong> water than outdoor farms. And there's another big advantage: less waste. On outdoor farms, some plants are damaged and farmers can't sell them — sometimes they have to throw away more than thirty per cent of the lettuce they grow.</p>
    <p>On this indoor farm, they throw away <strong>less than three per cent</strong>. Less water, less waste, more lettuce, faster.</p>
    <p>Would you eat lettuce grown under lights, with no sun and no soil? Many people in Japan already do.</p>
  </div>

